# Mito Data Studio — container deployment settings.
#
#   cp .env.docker.example .env.docker
#   # edit the REQUIRED block below
#   docker compose --env-file .env.docker up -d --build
#
# The --env-file flag is required on every compose command: without it compose
# reads `.env` (the host-development config) instead. See docker-compose.yml.
#
# This file is read by BOTH docker compose (for build args and the postgres
# service) and the app container (as env_file). It is separate from `.env`,
# which configures a host checkout — do not point the container at that one.
#
# .env.docker is gitignored. Never commit it.
#
# Only the settings that matter for a container deployment are listed here. The
# full catalogue, with explanations, is in .env.example.


# ============================================================================
# REQUIRED — the deployment will not start, or will not be secure, without them
# ============================================================================

# Django cryptographic key. Sessions, password-reset tokens and signed cookies
# all derive from it. Generate a fresh one per deployment; changing it later
# logs everyone out.
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(64))"
DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=

# PostgreSQL password. Also creates the database user on first `up`, so pick it
# before the first run — changing it afterwards needs a manual ALTER ROLE (or
# `docker compose down -v`, which deletes the database).
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
MITO_DB_PASSWORD=

# Hostnames this deployment answers to. Django rejects any request whose Host
# header is not listed. Add your real domain; keep localhost for health checks.
DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1

# Where the app's data root lives ON THE HOST — image volumes, labels,
# submissions, per-volume artifacts. Bind-mounted to /data in the container.
# Point it at a disk with room for your volumes; ./data is only a starting
# default. This directory IS your data: back it up, and never `down -v` it away.
MITO_HOST_DATA_DIR=./data


# ============================================================================
# Serving
# ============================================================================

# Host port. The container is bound to 127.0.0.1 in docker-compose.yml; front
# it with a TLS-terminating reverse proxy for anything public.
MITO_HOST_PORT=8000

# Never true on a deployment that holds real data — DEBUG leaks settings,
# SQL and stack traces to anyone who can trigger an error.
DJANGO_DEBUG=false

# Behind an HTTPS reverse proxy, uncomment these so Django knows the outside
# world is on TLS (otherwise redirects and secure cookies misbehave).
# DJANGO_CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://mito.example.org
# DJANGO_CORS_ORIGINS=https://mito.example.org
# MITO_TRUST_PROXY_SSL_HEADER=1
# MITO_SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=1
# MITO_CSRF_COOKIE_SECURE=1
# MITO_SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT=1

# gunicorn sizing. Workers are processes: roughly (2 x cores + 1), but each one
# holds its own AI model when the ai-* profiles are in use — 2 is a saner
# ceiling there, and on a single GPU more workers mostly means more VRAM.
# Leave unset to use the image defaults (3 workers / 2 threads), or run
# detect-hardware.sh --apply before the first build. Uncomment to override.
# GUNICORN_WORKERS=3
# GUNICORN_THREADS=2
# Slice reads touch multi-gigabyte TIFFs; the 30s gunicorn default kills them.
GUNICORN_TIMEOUT=300

# When 1, the entrypoint probes CPU/RAM/GPU and sets unset sizing variables.
# Preview on the host: ops/docker/detect-hardware.sh
# Seed an env file:      ops/docker/detect-hardware.sh --apply .env.docker
MITO_HARDWARE_AUTO_TUNE=0


# ============================================================================
# Build options
# ============================================================================

# Dependency profile baked into the image:
#   core    (default)  ~570 MB. No torch/ONNX. Annotation, viewing, export and
#                      sharing all work; AI-assist tools report unavailable and
#                      tracking uses the 'local' provider.
#   ai-cpu             ~3 GB.  EfficientSAM + SAM2 on CPU. Complete but slow.
#   ai-gpu             ~8 GB.  CUDA 12.4. Use the `gpu` compose profile.
# MITO_DEPS=core

# Which npm script compiles the SPA. Must agree with MITO_UPGRADE_PROFILE
# below — see "Upgrade profile" further down before changing either.
#   build           pairs with MITO_UPGRADE_PROFILE=legacy  (the default)
#   build:no-demo   same, minus the demo-account UI
#   build:production pairs with production_integrated_v1    (advanced, see below)
FRONTEND_BUILD_SCRIPT=build

# uid/gid the container runs as. Set these to the owner of MITO_HOST_DATA_DIR
# (`id -u` / `id -g`) or bind-mounted writes fail with permission errors.
APP_UID=1000
APP_GID=1000

# Model weights on the host, mounted read-only at /vendor. Only meaningful for
# the ai-* profiles, and requires `git lfs pull` to have fetched them.
MITO_HOST_VENDOR_DIR=./vendor


# ============================================================================
# Database (names only — host/port/engine are set by docker-compose.yml)
# ============================================================================

MITO_DB_NAME=mito
MITO_DB_USER=mito


# ============================================================================
# Application behaviour
# ============================================================================

# --- Upgrade profile -------------------------------------------------------
#
# `legacy` is the right choice for essentially every new deployment: it is the
# only profile with no additional runtime contract, so the app starts from the
# settings in this file alone.
#
# The alternative, `production_integrated_v1`, is NOT a "more production" knob.
# It is an audited contract for one specific two-GPU host, and
# backend/core/checks.py refuses to start the app unless every clause holds:
# PostgreSQL, a non-empty MITO_METRICS_BEARER_TOKEN, an empty
# MITO_PROCESSING_ENV_ALLOWLIST, SAM2 pinned to CUDA device 0 with EfficientSAM
# on device 1, and SAM2 + EfficientSAM weight files matching exact byte sizes.
# Selecting it without all of that gives deployment.E029/E030 and a container
# that restarts forever. Set FRONTEND_BUILD_SCRIPT=build:production with it.
MITO_UPGRADE_PROFILE=legacy

# The chunk transport and renderer are compiled into the SPA, so these describe
# what FRONTEND_BUILD_SCRIPT actually built — the backend cross-checks them and
# raises deployment.E024/E025 on a mismatch. `build` compiles neither, so both
# stay off here. Only turn them on together with a matching build script.
# VITE_FEATURE_CHUNK_PULL_QUEUE=true
# VITE_FEATURE_CHUNK_RENDERER=true

# Which tracking provider the Track tool uses. 'sam2' needs an ai-* image AND
# mounted weights; it logs a warning and falls back to 'local' otherwise, so
# leaving it set on a core image is harmless.
MITO_TRACKING_PROVIDER=sam2

# --- Track batch / SAM2 hardware tuning ------------------------------------
# Leave these unset for code defaults, or seed them with detect-hardware.sh.
# Explicit uncommented values always win over runtime auto-tuning.
# MITO_SAM2_CUDA_DEVICE=0
# MITO_AI_CUDA_DEVICE=1          # second GPU for EfficientSAM when present
# MITO_TRACK_PLAN_MAX_VOXELS=256000000
# MITO_SAM2_XY_PAD=256
# MITO_SAM2_XY_MIN=512
# MITO_SAM2_XY_MAX=2048
# OMP_NUM_THREADS=8

# Optional first-run admin. Both must be set for the account to be created; it
# is never overwritten on later starts, so changing the password here does
# nothing — use `docker compose exec app manage changepassword <user>`.
# Clear these once the account exists.
# DJANGO_SUPERUSER_USERNAME=admin
# DJANGO_SUPERUSER_EMAIL=admin@example.org
# DJANGO_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=

# Shared-password demo logins. Intended for throwaway demo instances only —
# leave disabled on anything holding real annotations.
ENABLE_MOCK_DEV_LOGIN=false
MOCK_DEV_LOGIN_ACCOUNTS=
MOCK_DEV_LOGIN_PASSWORD=

# /readyz reports not-ready below this much free disk — running out of space
# mid-write corrupts label files. 5 GB is a floor suited to a small instance;
# raise it toward the 50 GB (53687091200) the production deployment uses once
# you are storing real volumes.
MITO_READY_MIN_FREE_BYTES=5368709120

# Backend feature flags. FEATURE_CHUNK_SERVICE requires FEATURE_VOLUME_PYRAMIDS
# (deployment.E023), so move them together.
FEATURE_VOLUME_PYRAMIDS=1
FEATURE_CHUNK_SERVICE=1

# --- Mitochondria instance annotation, project management, quality ----------
# All four default to 0 so an existing deployment upgrades to identical
# behaviour and opts in one feature at a time. Every route is registered
# unconditionally and returns 503 when its flag is off, so a misconfiguration
# reads as "not enabled" rather than as a 404 typo.

# Per-instance morphology and QA flags (the Instance details panel in Annotate,
# plus the project Quality tab's instance summary). Purely additive: an absent
# row means "not annotated", which is what every existing volume already has.
FEATURE_INSTANCE_ANNOTATION=0

# The per-person inbox (the navbar bell and /inbox).
# NOTE: this gates the READ endpoints only. Notifications are written by the
# service layer regardless, so enabling this later reveals the history that
# accumulated rather than starting from an empty inbox.
FEATURE_NOTIFICATIONS=0

# Milestones plus the burndown / throughput / people / attention analytics on
# the project Delivery tab. Progress is recomputed from tasks on every read and
# never stored, so it cannot drift from the work it describes.
FEATURE_MILESTONES=0

# Dice/IoU and instance-level scoring (false merges, false splits, VI).
# Two sources, neither of which costs extra annotation: a gold-standard volume
# scored against an already-approved submission, and the difference between what
# an annotator submitted and what a reviewer actually approved.
FEATURE_QUALITY_METRICS=0
# How many recent gold-standard scores feed a person's rolling quality figure.
# Somebody with none reports "not measured", never 0.0.
# MITO_QUALITY_SCORE_WINDOW=10
# Mean Dice below this raises a quality.flagged notification to the managers.
# MITO_QUALITY_FLAG_THRESHOLD=0.80

# Two notification commands need a scheduler; neither has a triggering action.
#   daily:  python manage.py notify_deadlines --days 3
#   weekly: python manage.py prune_notifications --days 90
# The first is idempotent per (recipient, target, day), so an hourly cron
# cannot duplicate rows. The second deletes only *read* notifications past the
# window — an unread one is still owed to somebody, however old.
# See docs/deployment.md#scheduled-notification-tasks.
# Scoring runs from the service layer (on submit, and on approve), not from the
# QC provider registry — so MITO_QC_PROVIDER keeps its existing meaning and
# does not need changing for any of this.
